6171	zis365	69	0	instruments_tools	The concept addressed is perceived individual experiences of discrimination in everyday life as assessed by the Everyday Discrimination Scale. A German Arabic and Dari adaptation of the scale by Williams et al. 1997 is presented which underwent cognitive pretesting revision and validation in two samples a probability individual register sample for German and Arabic and a convenience sample recruited via Facebook for Arabic and Dari . The studies targeted the German speaking population and individuals with a migration background or refugees proficient in Arabic or Dari aged 18 years or older. Confirmatory Factor Analyses CFAs supported the unidimensional structure of the instrument. Validity and reliability analyses support its use within each language group and heterogeneous samples. Measurement invariance analyses showed that correlations can be compared without restrictions and means can be compared on the basis of partial invariance models.
